# ThreadSanitizer suppressions — ChronoKV
#
# Wired via TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=${{ github.workspace }}/docs/
# tsan.supp" in the ci.yml tsan legs (full-suite and public-api-smoke)
# and required for local `./build/tsan/test` runs. The path MUST be
# ABSOLUTE: libtsan resolves a relative suppressions path against the
# BINARY's directory, not the process CWD (measured: the first CI cut
# died on "failed to read suppressions file '.../build/tsan/docs/
# tsan.supp'"). Suppression TYPES are per-report-class by construction:
# a `deadlock:` entry can never suppress a data-race report — the race
# detector stays at full strength.
#
# =====================================================================
# deadlock:chronokv_btree::LatchTable — the page-latch ROLE-FLIP artifact
# =====================================================================
# Discovered by: the M4 landing CI run (2026-10-20, run 39468873357 —
# all three tsan legs red with 18-21 distinct "lock-order-inversion
# (potential deadlock)" cycles and 16 suite checks failing as
# `child exit 66`: forked battery children PASSED their checks but TSan
# overwrote their exit(0) with its exitcode 66 on report emission).
#
# Root cause (identity conflation, not a deadlock):
#   * LatchTable entries are keyed by PageId and NEVER erased — a
#     deliberate, argued design (M3 slice-3 decision-doc checklist item
#     7: erasing on free is WRONG under the immediate-return free list
#     because get_or_create hands out raw mutex pointers).
#   * v29 M4 gave PagePool::free() its first production callers, so a
#     slot's rwlock OBJECT now legitimately plays different tree roles
#     over time: ancestor of Y at t1 (descent records edge X=>Y), freed,
#     re-allocated as a DESCENDANT of Y at t2 (descent records Y=>X).
#     The lock-order graph accumulates both directions across time and
#     reports a cycle. Pre-M4 this was unobservable: production code
#     never freed a page, so a latch identity never flipped roles.
#   * Cross-instance heap-address reuse (the suite opens/closes hundreds
#     of Databases; freed LatchTable nodes' addresses are recycled) adds
#     the same class of stitched edges between unrelated mutex objects.
#
# Why it cannot deadlock (the argument this suppression rests on):
#   * The reported edge pairs are SHARED acquisitions (rdlock) — two
#     shared latches are mutually compatible; a shared/shared "cycle"
#     cannot block.
#   * Every descent (find_leaf_crabbing*, find_parent_crabbing, Cursor)
#     acquires page latches strictly TOP-DOWN against the CURRENT shape.
#   * Shape changes (split trims, merges, collapse) run under write_mu_
#     (single structural writer) and hold path EXCLUSIVES; a stale-shape
#     reader holds shared latches that the shape change must wait for,
#     completes against a consistent old shape, and releases — the two
#     directions can never be simultaneously held-and-waited.
#   * The decision doc's §1.2/§3.4 latch rules (never acquire upward,
#     siblings try-lock) are enforced in maybe_rebalance by construction.
#
# Residual blind spot (recorded, not hidden): a REAL inversion between a
# page latch and an ENGINE mutex (nm_/write_mu_/commit_mu/checkpoint_mu_/
# batch path) would also carry a LatchTable frame and be suppressed. Its
# backstop is behavioral: a real latch-order deadlock hangs the suite —
# the forked harnesses' parent deadlines and the CI job timeouts fail
# loudly (the C1 precedent). Engine-mutex-only cycles (no LatchTable
# frame) remain fully detected.
#
# Fail-first provenance (house norm): the artifact reproduces
# deterministically in ~20 s with a scratch driver — build 400 keys,
# drain-to-empty (merges + collapse free the root/leaf slots), regrow
# (LIFO slot reuse flips roles), descend: 2 lock-order reports, exit 66,
# oracles green (found=400 scan=400). With this suppression: 0 reports,
# exit 0, oracles unchanged. The 20-line driver is quoted in the fix
# commit message; the suite's own m4 D/G controls are the in-tree
# permanent shape (drain-to-empty + collapse-x-reuse).
#
# Alternative dispositions considered and rejected:
#   * erase latch entries on free — the exact WRONG the M3 decision doc
#     recorded (raw-pointer UAF window between get_or_create and the
#     latch acquisition in Cursor::enter_leaf's predecessor-released
#     gap);
#   * generation-keyed latch entries — unbounded entry growth under
#     churn, against P1's bounded-steady-state spirit;
#   * detect_deadlocks=0 — strictly broader (kills engine-mutex cycle
#     detection too); this file is the narrower cut.
deadlock:chronokv_btree::LatchTable
